/* Original fictional teaching examples. No answers are inserted into projects. */
(()=>{
 const lessons=[
  ['Scope a manageable topic','Technology affects everyone.','I will investigate how first-year commuters describe using travel time for study at one college.','A group, setting and phenomenon make access and boundaries discussable. This wording still needs literature review and a feasible recruitment plan.','Choose a topic in your own discipline. Which group and situation can you realistically reach?','Use your topic statement to decide what sources to search for; do not select a method just because it is familiar.'],
  ['Separate a problem from an assumption','Students perform badly because they are lazy.','Some students report difficulty planning study time. I need credible evidence of the issue and its context before proposing an explanation.','A judgment about people is not evidence of a research problem. Describe what is known, whose account it is and what remains uncertain.','What observation would challenge your initial explanation? Where could you find it?','Record the supporting source and a boundary; avoid presenting your motivation as a demonstrated finding.'],
  ['Make a search inspectable','I searched online and found relevant articles.','I will record database, date, exact query, filters and inclusion reasons, then revise the search after inspecting missing concepts.','An inspectable search helps another person understand what you looked for. A tidy search log does not make a search systematic or exhaustive.','Write synonyms for your two main concepts. Which disciplines or terms might your first query miss?','Keep rejected-source reasons where they matter. Search results and citation counts do not establish relevance or quality.'],
  ['Interpret disagreement','Three papers agree, so their result must be true.','Two studies report an association, while a third does not. I will compare populations, measures, timing and uncertainty before explaining the difference.','Counting agreeing papers ignores design and measurement differences. Conflicting findings may reflect context, sampling, bias or imprecision; they need not be errors.','Suppose one study measures self-reported study hours and another uses a diary. What comparison can you defend?','Code both supporting and challenging evidence. Preserve source-specific limitations when building a theme.'],
  ['Justify a provisional gap','Nobody has studied this topic.','In the sources reviewed so far, commuter students at this setting are underrepresented; I will broaden the search before claiming this is an important gap.','An unsuccessful search does not establish absence. A useful gap also explains why resolving the uncertainty matters. Replication may be worthwhile even when studies exist.','If you discover a closely related study tomorrow, how would you revise the contribution?','Use bounded language: name the literature examined and the uncertainty your project can realistically address.'],
  ['Match the question to the claim','Does screen time cause low grades? I will use one survey.','Among the sampled students, is reported screen time associated with reported grades?','A one-time observational survey cannot by itself establish causation. Reverse direction, confounding and reporting error remain possible.','Could workload influence both variables? Name another plausible explanation and what evidence would distinguish it.','Align the claim with the design. More participants alone do not resolve confounding or establish cause.'],
  ['Make objectives do useful work','To understand everything about student wellbeing.','To describe participants’ accounts of study planning and examine recurring challenges and differences within those accounts.','An objective should specify an achievable contribution. For qualitative work, a useful objective need not be a numerical measurement or hypothesis test.','For your question, what would a completed objective produce: an estimate, an interpretation, a comparison or an evaluated artifact?','Check that every objective has a justified evidence source and analysis plan; remove objectives you cannot support.'],
  ['Explain sampling and analysis choices','I will survey whoever replies and generalize to all students.','I will recruit accessible volunteers, report how they were reached and describe the sample. Any wider inference will be limited by coverage and self-selection.','Convenience is an access constraint, not a representativeness argument. No universal sample-size rule fits every design: justify precision or power for relevant quantitative aims and information needs for qualitative aims.','Who is least likely to participate? How could that absence change your interpretation?','For quantitative work, define variables, missing-data handling and uncertainty before interpreting a test. For qualitative work, explain the analytic approach, reflexivity and how interpretations are developed. Seek specialist review where needed.'],
  ['Audit the whole argument','My proposal has all sections, so it is ready.','I will trace each objective through the evidence needed, recruitment, collection, analysis and permitted claim, then document unresolved access and ethics questions.','Filled sections can still contradict each other. A coherent proposal explains its choices, feasibility and limits; institutional approval is a separate process.','If recruitment yields fewer or different participants than planned, which claims and steps would need revision?','Use Review to find missing records, then ask a supervisor to assess the reasoning. Preserve uncertainties rather than filling them with unsupported promises.']
 ];
 const esc=s=>String(s).replace(/[&<>"']/g,c=>({'&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;'}[c]));
 function content(i){const x=lessons[i];return `<h3>${esc(x[0])}</h3><p class="lab-fiction">Fictional teaching wording. Adapt the reasoning to your discipline and real evidence.</p><h4>A weak starting point</h4><p>${esc(x[1])}</p><h4>A more defensible revision</h4><p>${esc(x[2])}</p><h4>Why the revision helps</h4><p>${esc(x[3])}</p><h4>Try a different situation</h4><p>${esc(x[4])}</p><h4>Carry this into your project</h4><p>${esc(x[5])}</p><p>No wording here certifies a study design. Use institutional guidance and supervisor review, especially for ethics, sample-size planning and specialist analysis.</p>`}
 function mount(){
  const parts=location.hash.slice(1).split('/');
  if(parts[0]==='lesson'&&(!parts[2]||parts[2]==='write')){const host=document.querySelector('#exercise-text')?.closest('.lesson-content,.lesson-main,.writing-studio,.lesson-body,.exercise')||document.querySelector('.lesson-content,.lesson-main,.lesson-body');const i=Number(parts[1]);if(host&&lessons[i]&&!host.querySelector('[data-reasoning]'))host.insertAdjacentHTML('beforeend',`<details class="learning-lab" data-reasoning><summary>Improve the reasoning: ${esc(lessons[i][0])}</summary>${content(i)}</details>`)}
  const roomLessons={library:2,evidence:3,claims:5,synthesis:3,design:7,proposal:8,review:8};const i=roomLessons[parts[2]];
  if(parts[0]==='research'&&i!==undefined){const host=document.querySelector('.research-content');if(host&&!host.querySelector('[data-reasoning]'))host.insertAdjacentHTML('beforeend',`<details class="learning-lab" data-reasoning><summary>Reasoning clinic: ${esc(lessons[i][0])}</summary>${content(i)}<a href="#lesson/${i}">Open the related lesson</a></details>`)}
  const heading=document.querySelector('.research-project-heading');if(heading&&!heading.querySelector('[data-jump-work]'))heading.insertAdjacentHTML('afterbegin','<button type="button" class="text-button" data-jump-work>Jump to working area</button>');
 }
 document.addEventListener('click',event=>{if(!event.target.closest('[data-jump-work]'))return;const area=document.querySelector('.research-content');if(area){area.tabIndex=-1;area.focus({preventScroll:true});area.scrollIntoView({block:'start',behavior:'instant'})}});
 addEventListener('rrh:route',()=>{mount();queueMicrotask(mount)});addEventListener('rei:research-render',mount);
 window.REIReasoning={lessons};
})();
